{"name":"Gulf Stream Blue","hex":"#689fc1","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"gulf","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A hollow place in the earth; an abyss; a deep chasm or basin.","That which swallows; the gullet."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To engulf.","To award a degree to somebody who has only just passed sufficiently."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/gulf"},{"term":"stream","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A small river; a large creek; a body of moving water confined by banks.","All moving waters."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To flow in a continuous or steady manner, like a liquid.","To extend; to stretch out with a wavy motion; to float in the wind."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/stream"},{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Gulf Stream","blocks":[{"text":"The Gulf Stream is a warm and swift Atlantic ocean current that originates in the Gulf of Mexico and flows through the Straits of Florida and up the eastern coastline of the United States, then veers east near 36°N latitude and moves toward Northwest Europe as the North Atlantic Current. The process of western intensification causes the Gulf Stream to be a northward-accelerating current off the east coast of North America. Around 40°0′N 30°0′W, it splits in two, with the northern stream, the North Atlantic Drift, crossing to Northern Europe and the southern stream, the Canary Current, recirculating off West Africa."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/19/Golfstrom.jpg/330px-Golfstrom.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gulf_Stream","wikidata":"Q130905"},{"title":"Stream","blocks":[{"text":"A stream is a continuous body of surface water flowing within the bed and banks of a channel. The channel is a watercourse, although the watercourse may be seasonally or periodically dry. Depending on its location or certain characteristics, a stream may be referred to by a variety of local or regional names. A long, large stream is usually called a river, while a smaller, less voluminous, and more intermittent stream is known as a creek or tributary, among other names."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9c/%D0%A6%D0%B2%D1%80%D1%86%D0%BA%D0%B0_2_PPRP001.jpg/330px-%D0%A6%D0%B2%D1%80%D1%86%D0%BA%D0%B0_2_PPRP001.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stream","wikidata":"Q47521"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue","wikidata":"Q1088"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/19/Golfstrom.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/19/Golfstrom.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"Surface temperatures in the western North Atlantic: The Gulf Stream is represented by the large red-yellow (hot) flow from the Gulf of Mexico northwards in this image. Source: NASA","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGolfstrom.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ec/Gulfstream.jpg/500px-Gulfstream.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ec/Gulfstream.jpg/500px-Gulfstream.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Evolution of the Gulf Stream to the west of Ireland continuing as the North Atlantic Current","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGulfstream.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bd/Sandy_2012-10-28_1600Z.jpg/500px-Sandy_2012-10-28_1600Z.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bd/Sandy_2012-10-28_1600Z.jpg/1280px-Sandy_2012-10-28_1600Z.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bd/Sandy_2012-10-28_1600Z.jpg/500px-Sandy_2012-10-28_1600Z.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Hurricane Sandy intensified as it tracked northward along the axis of the Gulf Stream in 2012.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASandy_2012-10-28_1600Z.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9c/%D0%A6%D0%B2%D1%80%D1%86%D0%BA%D0%B0_2_PPRP001.jpg/500px-%D0%A6%D0%B2%D1%80%D1%86%D0%BA%D0%B0_2_PPRP001.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_cont